---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/architecture/intermediate/plans-sections-and-scale">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate architecture lesson. It assumes the foundational lesson <em>Form
    Follows Forces</em>, so that walls, spans and loads mean something to you; the sibling
    intermediate <em>What Buildings Must Do</em> pairs well with this one, because drawings
    are where those obligations get fixed in size and position. No software or drawing
    experience is needed: this is a reading lesson as much as a making one.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="find the faulty line — the dimension string" widget="spot-error" config={psStringSpot} />
  <Widget no="3" caption="pair each drawing with what it exists to show" widget="match" config={psSetMatch} />
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>name the parts of a drawing set - plans, sections, elevations, details - and say what each exists to show;</li>
    <li>explain orthographic projection and why its conventions amount to a truth guarantee;</li>
    <li>choose a scale to match a decision and convert real dimensions at 1:100, 1:50 and 1:5;</li>
    <li>read a plan as choreography: entrance sequence, circulation, adjacencies and light;</li>
    <li>dimension and annotate so a site crew can actually build - tolerances, datums, schedules.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Architectural drawing is <strong>a precision language for unbuilt things</strong> —
    orthographic cuts that tell the truth about size and position, at scales chosen to match the
    decisions each drawing carries. Reading drawings is literacy; making them readable is the
    craft. Like any language it has grammar (projection, line weight, convention), vocabulary
    (the drawing types) and register (the scale you choose encodes how much you have decided).
    Learning it does two things at once: it lets you read any building document ever drawn —
    the convention set is centuries old and nearly global — and it teaches the architect's
    deepest habit, that <em>deciding is drawing</em>: until a thing has a dimension, a level
    and a junction, it is a wish, not a design.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the set, the scales, the choreography, the dimensions" widget="equationStep" config={planCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four stages are the drawing set in miniature - the
    database, the scale ladder, the social script, the dimensions. Watch each stage add precision of
    commitment: by stage 4, every line carries a number a builder is owed.
  </p>

  <h2>Orthographic projection: the truthful cut</h2>
  <p>
    A photograph lies to builders (distance shrinks; occlusion hides); orthographic
    projection does not. The idea: view the building along one axis and record true sizes in
    that view's plane, discarding depth. The <strong>plan</strong> is a horizontal cut —
    conventionally about 1.2 m above the floor, so it slices through walls, door leaves and
    window openings but under ceilings — projected straight down. The <strong>section</strong>
    is a vertical cut: what the building looks like if you slice it and remove the near half.
    The <strong>elevation</strong> is a view without a cut: each facade flat and true. The
    mathematical machinery behind this was formalised by Gaspard Monge as <em>descriptive
    geometry</em> in the 1780s–90s — valuable enough that the French military classified it a
    state secret before publication, because it let fortifications be designed and
    fabricated from paper alone. Architecture adopted it wholesale: with plan, section and
    elevation, every length in a building is either drawn at true size or derivable from
    two drawings that are. That is the language's core promise: <strong>nothing is
    ambiguous</strong> — or, if it is, that ambiguity is now a drawn, checkable fact.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="bet first — Monge's classified geometry" widget="predict" config={psMongePredict} />

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> orthographic projection makes drawings truthful - every length
    true in its plane or derivable from two drawings, nothing ambiguous. Truth at full size is not yet truth at
    drawing size; the next section is the conversion that carries decisions.
  </p>

  <h2>Scale: a zoom level into consequences</h2>
  <p>
    The scale fractions are a decision ladder. <strong>1:1000–1:2500</strong> (urban and site
    location plans): one metre is a millimetre — decisions about where the building sits and
    how it meets the street. <strong>1:200–1:500</strong> (site plans): drainage falls, parking,
    tree root zones. <strong>1:100</strong> (the workhorse general-arrangement plan): rooms,
    wall thicknesses, door swings — 900 mm of door becomes 9 mm of line, and a 60 mm wall
    versus a 100 mm wall is the difference between a partition and a lining question.
    <strong>1:50</strong> (sections, bathrooms, stairs): fittings, head heights, the tread-riser
    arithmetic of a staircase (two risers plus one tread ≈ 630 mm — the comfort rule). 
    <strong>1:20–1:1</strong> (details): the window head, the roof edge, the rainscreen clip —
    where water is defeated. The teaching point the widget makes is exactly right: the same
    wall is a 3-second line at 1:200 and a twenty-minute negotiation at 1:5, because at 1:5
    the wall is an assembly of layers — structure, insulation, vapour control, cavity,
    cladding — each with a thickness that physically cannot sum to zero. <strong>Scale is
    therefore an honesty mechanism</strong>: you cannot claim at 1:100 what a 1:5 detail will
    contradict, and experienced reviewers read plans knowing which claims have not yet met
    their details.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="fill the blanks — the workhorse scale and the comfort rule" widget="cloze" config={psScaleCloze} />

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "any scale will do to start - you can always zoom in
  later." Scale is not magnification; it is a commitment level. A wall that is a casual line
  at 1:200 is a stack of physical layers at 1:5, and a design that only works on the coarse
  drawing has not been designed - experienced reviewers read plans precisely to find which
  claims have not yet met their details.</p>

  <h2>Reading a plan: choreography in ink</h2>
  <p>
    A plan is drawn as solid and void, but it is written as a <strong>social script</strong>.
    Start where the building does: the entrance. What sequence follows — compression
    (a low, dark lobby) then release (the double-height stair with the view)? That
    arrival choreography is deliberate, the same suspense structure a film editor
    builds. Then trace the unavoidable paths: the <strong>circulation</strong> — corridors,
    stairs, lifts — and ask what they force strangers to share and family members to
    repeat. Then the <strong>adjacencies</strong>: kitchen to dining (carrying hot pans),
    bedroom to bathroom (night trips, acoustic buffering), staff corridors crossing
    clean ones in a hospital (infection control as a floor plan). Then the light:
    which rooms have which orientations, and whether the plan's deepest points —
    the parts farthest from a window (roughly 2–2.5× window-head-height is the
    daylight rule of thumb) — are service spaces or someone's desk. The
    <strong>poché</strong> — cut walls filled solid black — is the reading aid that makes
    all of this legible at a glance: solid against void, structure against space, with
    line weights ranking the drawing's truths (cut elements heaviest, nearest objects
    heavier than distant ones). Graphic convention here is not decoration; it is
    compression.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="rebuild the plan-reading sequence" widget="order" config={psReadOrder} />

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> a plan reads as a social script - entrance, compression, release,
    the paths people cannot avoid - with poché and line weights compressing it into one glance. What a
    plan cannot show is the next section's subject: height.
  </p>

  <h2>Sections: the vertical argument</h2>
  <p>
    Plans hide the most expensive dimension: height. The <strong>section</strong> shows
    floor-to-floor heights, the structural depth of spans (a long-span roof eats its own
    half-metre of section), stair construction, light wells, double-height voids and the
    stacking logic of services. Many great buildings are <em>section-first</em> designs: a
    Gothic nave, a concert hall, a top-lit gallery — the section is the architectural idea,
    and the plan merely serves it. Reading a section like an engineer too: where does the
    rain go (falls, outlets, upstands), where does the vapour go (the thermal envelope's
    continuity is a sectional question), where do the pipes run (vertical risers stacked
    floor above floor save metres of copper and days of labour). A plan can lie about a
    building's difficulty; a section mostly cannot.
  </p>

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the plan is the main drawing; sections are supporting
  material." For many buildings the order reverses: a Gothic nave, a concert hall or a
  top-lit gallery is a section-first design, and the plan merely serves it. A plan can lie
  about a building's difficulty; a section mostly cannot.</p>

  <h2>Details, dimensions and tolerances: where drawing meets reality</h2>
  <p>
    Between drawing and building sits the tolerance — every process has an accuracy it
    cannot beat, and designs that ignore it fail on site. Site-formed concrete is honest to
    about ±10 mm, precast panels to a few millimetres, steelwork to ±3 mm or so, joinery to
    ±1 mm. A competent detail is a <strong>tolerance stack</strong>: it asks what happens
    when everything drifts to the edge of its band, and it usually answers with a
    deliberately generous joint — the gap that absorbs everyone's error (the shadow-gap at
    a cladding edge is tolerance dressed as aesthetics). <strong>Dimensioning</strong> has
    its own discipline: dimension to faces you can set out from, avoid chained errors, and
    close the strings — segment dimensions must sum exactly to the overall, or the 10 mm
    of rounding you left in the spreadsheet becomes a bricklayer's improvised judgement.
    Drawings also speak in <strong>annotations</strong> (materials, finishes, levels — the
    datum symbols that say "this floor is +0.150", fixing every height in the building to
    one origin) and <strong>schedules</strong> (doors and windows counted in tables, each
    with a type mark that links plan, elevation and specification). And beneath it all
    sits <strong>buildability</strong>: every junction a real crew can actually build in
    the drawn sequence, in the rain, with the crane they have. A detail that is beautiful
    and unbuildable is a model, not a design.
  </p>

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "a professional drawing specifies exact sizes, and
  tolerances are for sloppy work." Every process has an accuracy it cannot beat - site-formed
  concrete to about plus or minus 10 mm, joinery to about 1 mm - so a drawing that pretends
  otherwise just relocates the error somewhere unplanned. The competent detail is a tolerance
  stack with a deliberately generous joint that absorbs everyone's drift.</p>

  <h2>From lines to database: BIM and the surviving sketch</h2>
  <p>
    Building Information Modelling changed the medium without changing the language: the
    project is built first as a coordinated 3-D model whose every object carries data —
    fire rating, U-value, cost, maintenance interval — and the drawings become projections
    and queries of that model. The immediate dividend is <strong>clash detection</strong>:
    the software intersects every duct with every beam and every pipe with every soffit,
    catching on Tuesday the collision that used to be discovered on site by a worker with
    a hammer and a change order. The deeper dividend is coordination across the whole
    team — structure, services, envelope building in one model — which is how a modern
    hospital (miles of services threaded through a structural grid) is even possible.
    Yet the pre-digital tool survives where it wins: the <strong>diagram sketch</strong> —
    massing blocks, a circulation arrow, a section scribble — still moves from intent to
    evaluation faster than any software, and the discipline's notebooks remain its
    laboratories. The division of labour that emerged is a sane one: machines coordinate
    and check; humans conceive and decide.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="bet first — what clash detection actually catches" widget="predict" config={psClashPredict} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the plans are the building." Drawings are a
  communication protocol — a lossless one only if you read the whole set together. A plan
  without its section is wrong about heights; a section without details is wrong about
  materials; the specification (the written book of materials and workmanship) outranks
  the drawings where they conflict. Professionals read sets, never sheets.</p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="true or false — the language's fine print" widget="judge" config={psSetJudge} />

  <h2>Surveys: where the drawing meets the earth</h2>
  <p>
    Every drawing set rests on a <strong>survey</strong> — the measured description of
    what already exists, because buildings begin on a planet that was there first.
    Topographic surveys record levels against a national datum (in Britain, Ordnance
    Datum Newlyn; elsewhere local benchmarks), so the "±0.000" of your ground-floor
    plan is a legal promise tied to a brass bolt somewhere. Site plans overlay the
    proposal on <strong>cadastral</strong> boundaries (the legal ownership lines — a
    200 mm encroachment can be litigation), existing drainage, and statutory
    services; modern surveys arrive as laser-scanned point clouds and orthorectified
    aerial imagery, with drone photogrammetry and LiDAR making millimetre-honest
    existing-conditions models routine. The professional discipline this creates:
    <strong>verify, never assume</strong> — measured surveys beat archive drawings
    (as-built records drift), and levels are checked twice because a 50 mm error at
    the site entrance becomes a ramp that does not drain and a door threshold in a
    puddle. The drawing set's first sheet is usually the least glamorous and the
    most consequential: get the earth wrong and every elegant plan above it is
    fiction with dimensions.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the drawing set stands on a measured earth - datums, boundaries,
    twice-checked levels - because a plan anchored to nothing is fiction with dimensions.
    Time to check that it holds together.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A site plan at 1:500 and a window-head detail at 1:5 both show the same wall. What different decisions does each drawing carry?</li>
    <li>Why does a 900 mm door leaf become 9 mm of line at 1:100, and what professional habit does the conversion support?</li>
    <li>What does a closed dimension string guarantee, and what happens on site when a string does not close?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) The 1:500 plan carries siting decisions - where the building
    meets the street, drainage falls, boundaries - while the 1:5 detail carries the junction
    where water is defeated; scale is a zoom level into consequences. (2) Divide by the scale
    denominator: 900 mm divided by 100 gives 9 mm; the habit it supports is checking every
    drawn distance against what a body, a trolley or a wheelchair actually needs. (3) It
    guarantees the segment dimensions sum exactly to the overall; an open string leaves a
    rounding residue the crew must improvise somewhere - usually at the worst joint.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Orthographic projection is the truth guarantee: every length true in its plane or derivable from two drawings.</li>
    <li>Plan, section, elevation and details each answer a different question; read sets, never sheets.</li>
    <li>Scale is a commitment level - the same wall is a 3-second line at 1:200 and a negotiation at 1:5.</li>
    <li>A plan is a social script: entrance sequence, circulation, adjacencies, light.</li>
    <li>Sections show what plans hide - height, structural depth, services, the vertical argument.</li>
    <li>Details are tolerance stacks; dimension strings must close; generous joints absorb everyone's error.</li>
    <li>BIM coordinates and checks; humans still conceive and decide - which is why the sketch survives.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sibling lessons <em>What Buildings Must Do</em> and <em>A History of Architectural
    Styles</em> supply what the drawings are about - obligations and inherited forms -
    and the advanced <em>Structural Systems: Arches to Skyscrapers</em> lesson explains the
    depths those sections show.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Projection families and their honest uses.</strong> Besides orthographic cuts,
    the toolkit includes <strong>axonometric and isometric projections</strong> (true
    dimensions with all three axes visible — the analytical darlings, because no vanishing
    point distorts any part) and <strong>perspective</strong> (the perceptual truth — how
    the space will <em>feel</em> — with systematic size distortion along depth). The
    conventions map to questions: plan/section for decisions, axon for spatial reasoning,
    perspective for persuasion. Historic footnote worth knowing: what we call the
    "figured ground" <strong>figure–ground plan</strong> (buildings black, streets white)
    descends from Nolli's 1748 map of Rome, which drew public interiors as open ground —
    a claim about civic access drawn, not stated.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Standards are the multiplayer protocol.</strong> Line weights, level symbols,
    hatch conventions, sheet numbering and title blocks are standardised (ISO 128 in
    Europe, national CAD standards elsewhere) because drawing sets are read by hundreds of
    people downstream — engineers, contractors, inspectors, insurers — and a private
    dialect costs everyone money. The RIBA/AIA stage frameworks do the same for the
    process: they fix which decisions exist at which stage, so that scale choices and
    detail choices arrive in the right order (decide the stair position before the
    balustrade profile).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> The paperless site (tablets on the scaffold, models
    in the hoist cage) is mostly here; the <em>paperless decision</em> is not — liability
    and contract still run on issued documents. Generative and parametric tools can now
    propose layouts against rules (parking counts, daylight autonomy, fire egress); the
    open question is what the human's comparative advantage becomes when options are
    cheap — historically, the answer in every design field has been judgement about
    which problem is worth solving, which is why the sketch survived the database.
    And a live regulatory question: as models become the legal building record, the
    drawing language this page teaches is not dying but changing substrate — the
    orthographic cut, four centuries old, is likely to outlive the sheet of paper that
    carries it.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Ching, Building Construction Illustrated and Architectural Graphics; Monge, Géométrie descriptive (1799); RIBA drawing conventions; Salmon, Drawing for Architects; Nolli, Nuova Pianta di Roma (1748). Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="check yourself" widget="quiz" config={planQuizCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> commit to an answer before revealing the feedback - each
    explanation is the lesson's argument in miniature, and the wrong options are all
    plausible-sounding versions of the misconceptions this page dismantled.
  </p>
</TopicPage>
